CUDA Batch Image Processor

This project implements a GPU-accelerated batch image processing pipeline using CUDA C++. The goal is to demonstrate scalable processing of a large number of small image inputs while keeping the main pixel computation on the GPU.

The program generates or accepts a batch of PPM RGB images and applies grayscale conversion, with an optional inversion step. The central CUDA kernel assigns one GPU thread to each pixel. Each thread reads the RGB values, computes a weighted grayscale intensity, optionally inverts the value, and writes the result. The kernel is launched with a configurable number of threads per block.

I chose the PPM format to keep the project small and reproducible without adding an external image-processing dependency. This also makes it easy to generate a deterministic dataset containing 1,000 small images and to verify the input and output files.

The host program is responsible for command-line parsing, file I/O, CUDA memory allocation, data transfers, kernel launch, timing, and output generation. CUDA events are used to measure kernel execution time, while a wall-clock timer measures total application time. The program reports the GPU name, number of processed images, total pixels, kernel time, total execution time, and image throughput.

One challenge was separating GPU computation from file I/O. Reading and writing image files remains a CPU operation, while the computationally intensive per-pixel transformation is executed by the GPU. This makes the division of work explicit and provides measurable CUDA execution evidence.

The project also demonstrates how the same kernel can process a larger workload without changing the algorithm. Increasing the number of input images increases the amount of parallel pixel work, while the configurable thread-block size allows different launch configurations to be tested.

The main lesson from the project is that GPU acceleration is most useful when the workload contains many independent operations. Image pixels are naturally independent for this transformation, making the problem a good fit for CUDA's thread-based parallel execution model.

Execution artifacts should be generated by running the supplied script in a CUDA-enabled environment. The artifacts contain the execution log and representative input/output images.
